Correct the claim that production objects are small (P-280)

WHAT-THE-PRODUCT-IS said the production forest averages about 17 access control entries
per object against the laboratory's 25, and concluded production objects are smaller on
average. It is wrong by thirty times. --judgement-forecast measured
ad.ssa.gov-20260924-020431 directly: 330,856,576 permission occurrences across 659,196
objects carrying readable permissions is 502 entries per object, and the descriptors
themselves average 521. The owner confirmed the figure was wrong and believes it was a
laboratory number attached to the wrong forest.

The decision it was used to support - cutting bounded paging from P-249 - still stands,
because performance work belongs on judging and P-280 has since measured exactly how
much: a production judgement would be 136.5 GiB, of which 72% is one name copied three
times. That argument never needed the 17. But the reason given for it is deleted rather
than repaired, because nothing may be designed on the belief that an object carries a
couple of dozen entries, and if paging is ever reconsidered it must be against 502.

Also records the order the remaining work is being done in, which was settled with the
owner after the review: make the measurement honest first, because every later
measurement runs through the forecast and the false ContentKey comment is still there
for the next person to build on; then a bench that can disagree with us, since the
synthetic was generated by this codebase and could only hold assumptions the code
already held; then the two correctness prerequisites, P-283 and P-285; then P-280
itself.
